WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.969
-Ah, Cecily, it is great
to see you.

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:04.971
-Hello.
-Cecily, congrats!

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:07.573
-Great to see you.

00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:09.375
-Congrats, your first Emmy nom.

00:00:09.375 --> 00:00:11.110
Come on, how does it feel?

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:15.214
How'd you find out?
-It's nuts.

00:00:15.214 --> 00:00:19.218
I was home and I sort of --
I was, like, waiting around.

00:00:19.218 --> 00:00:21.420
I knew they were coming out
that day,

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.257
but I'm never expecting it,
so far from it.

00:00:25.257 --> 00:00:28.961
And, like, normally,
I would get -- Every other year

00:00:28.961 --> 00:00:32.231
people are really nice, but
they'd be like, "I'm so sorry."

00:00:32.231 --> 00:00:35.468
You're like, "Wha-- I don't --

00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:39.371
I appreciate that, but, like,
I was feeling fine today."

00:00:39.371 --> 00:00:43.476
So then I got a text from
our publicist, Lauren Rodsman,

00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:45.111
and she just said,

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:48.081
"Congrats, you were nominated"
or something,

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:52.051
and I was like, "For what?
What does that mean?"

00:00:52.051 --> 00:00:55.755
And then I really had to --
I did a lot of digging.

00:00:55.755 --> 00:01:00.226
I had my roommate, Kevin --
he's my quarantine buddy.

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:03.363
And I texted him,
"I think I was nominated."

00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:05.031
And then he ran out of the room.

00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:08.367
And so it took me like a day
to fully believe it.

00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:10.303
-You're like, "As a show
or as a cast?

00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:12.371
Did I write something?
Or is it a song?"

00:01:12.371 --> 00:01:15.474
-Right, the show gets nominated,
what is it that this is for?

00:01:15.474 --> 00:01:19.145
-You crushed it.
-Costumes at home?

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:22.148
-Oh, the at-home shows,
you were great on that as well.

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:25.051
-Those were some of the hardest
we've worked, I think.

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:29.588
I had to learn all
these weird technical programs.

00:01:29.588 --> 00:01:34.060
I'm 180. I'm like Tom Cruise
in "Interview with the Vampire."

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.595
-Oh, no, come on.

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:37.797
-That old when it comes
to technology.

00:01:37.797 --> 00:01:38.864
I miss the train.

00:01:38.864 --> 00:01:40.199
I miss that window.

00:01:40.199 --> 00:01:42.168
So trying to learn
all of these things.

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:44.403
And they sent us,
like, a full studio.

00:01:44.403 --> 00:01:48.274
I have all these mikes
and a full green screen

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:50.476
that we had to hang
from our stairway.

00:01:50.476 --> 00:01:52.212
-That's right.

00:01:52.212 --> 00:01:55.214
-Yeah, so that was --
it was really crazy.

00:01:55.214 --> 00:01:58.217
And then doing our
own hair and makeup --

00:01:58.217 --> 00:02:01.620
My friend Kevin made
beautiful props, though.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:02.989
I had him.

00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:05.057
-Is Kevin going to make you
props for the Emmys?

00:02:05.057 --> 00:02:07.026
How is that going to work?

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:08.194
-He's going to have to.

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:10.430
He'll be the only one
with me again. Yeah.

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:12.198
I don't know how that works.

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:15.969
You know, we're in
this asterisk year.

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:18.404
I don't have to wear Spanx,
I don't think.

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:20.640
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:21.874
You know, it's interesting.

00:02:21.874 --> 00:02:24.176
We were doing research,
and I saw that

00:02:24.176 --> 00:02:26.913
"Late Night" was
your first talk show.

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:28.380
It was the first season of "SNL"
for you.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.450
-Yes.
-See, I knew back then.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.619
I knew, see.
-Wow, yeah, yeah.

00:02:33.619 --> 00:02:35.187
-I was very prescient.

00:02:35.187 --> 00:02:38.124
I knew that you were gonna
get the Emmy nom.

00:02:38.124 --> 00:02:40.693
-I think you said it.
-Your ninth season.

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:41.727
[ Telephone rings ]

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:43.295
-Sorry, my house phone
is ringing.

00:02:43.295 --> 00:02:45.131
I don't know
who would even have this.

00:02:45.131 --> 00:02:47.099
-You just got nominated for
another Emmy.

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:48.568
That's what we're calling.

00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:50.102
[ Laughter ]

00:02:50.102 --> 00:02:51.637
-They're calling on
the land line.

00:02:51.637 --> 00:02:53.739
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, we just
wanted to get your reaction

00:02:53.739 --> 00:02:56.075
while we're Zooming, yeah.

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:59.612
Does your dad still suggest
characters to you?

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:04.117
-Yeah, so, I mean, he
definitely -- it's still in him.

00:03:04.117 --> 00:03:06.152
He tries to control it
a little bit.

00:03:06.152 --> 00:03:11.090
But I have -- Actually, I lost
my wonderful cousin Owen

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:14.593
to glioblastoma in January,

00:03:14.593 --> 00:03:21.267
and I wound up at the start of
corona, really, March,

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:25.705
I connected with
his neurooncologist from Duke

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:31.510
who was, like, the most
outspoken body man,

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:34.013
Dr. Friedman,
and he sends like --

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:38.684
we're all on a group text, and
he sends like 76 texts a day,

00:03:38.684 --> 00:03:41.921
editorial, news articles
and then some editorial,

00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:43.556
and he's always like,
"Now, I don't think

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:45.792
you want to use this for 'SNL.'"

00:03:45.792 --> 00:03:47.860
So, like, Henry's my new --

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.962
-Yeah.

00:03:49.962 --> 00:03:52.398
-I'm like, "Henry, aren't you
busy right now?"

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:54.333
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:56.869
-Your characters are so good.

00:03:56.869 --> 00:03:59.004
Are they based off
of real people?

00:03:59.004 --> 00:04:02.141
Where do you pull from?
Like the girl at the party?

00:04:02.141 --> 00:04:03.475
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:03.475 --> 00:04:06.845
I mean, Girl at the Party
started off

00:04:06.845 --> 00:04:08.481
just kind of making fun
of myself.

00:04:08.481 --> 00:04:12.718
And then it was kind of like
the beginning of social media,

00:04:12.718 --> 00:04:15.721
really being soapboxy,
I feel like,

00:04:15.721 --> 00:04:19.191
and I remember this girl
on Facebook

00:04:19.191 --> 00:04:21.727
when Obama won his second term.

00:04:21.727 --> 00:04:25.731
She wrote something like,
"Wow, everybody's really happy,

00:04:25.731 --> 00:04:29.202
and that makes me
really, really sad."

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:31.604
It was just such an insane thing
to say.

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:35.607
-Do Jost and Che know everything
you're going to say there?

00:04:35.607 --> 00:04:39.011
-No, so, I love now --

00:04:39.011 --> 00:04:41.747
the characters that we do are
pretty loose.

00:04:41.747 --> 00:04:43.583
And Colin's -- I'm like,

00:04:43.583 --> 00:04:45.317
"Colin, do you mind
if I throw up on you?"

00:04:45.317 --> 00:04:46.919
And he's like,
"Yeah, that sounds great."

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:48.154
[ Laughter ]

00:04:48.154 --> 00:04:50.923
-That was unbelievable.
That was so good.

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:53.059
-He loves it. He loves it.

00:04:53.059 --> 00:04:55.361
-He loves being thrown up on.

00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:56.729
-He does.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:05:01.200
He let himself be dunked on
at the home show.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.669
But I think, like, what's nice
about the characters is

00:05:03.669 --> 00:05:08.007
I really do feel like I get
a chance to kind of try to

00:05:08.007 --> 00:05:09.274
make them laugh, if I can.

00:05:09.274 --> 00:05:12.010
There was, like, a Cathy Ann
we did once,

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:15.014
and I said Mitch McDonald's --
that's what I call him --

00:05:15.014 --> 00:05:18.451
and Che kind of laughed
and said, "Mitch McDonald's?"

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:20.252
And I went, "Yeah!
You don't know Mitch McDonald,

00:05:20.252 --> 00:05:24.156
you're gonna run 'Update'?"
or something like that.

00:05:24.156 --> 00:05:25.891
There's always, like, moments.

00:05:25.891 --> 00:05:29.462
I think that's the best place
for being a little loose

00:05:29.462 --> 00:05:32.498
and getting to improvise
and making those guys laugh.

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:34.833
-I love that voice.
Already I'm laughing at it.

00:05:34.833 --> 00:05:36.635
Break a leg at the Emmys.

00:05:36.635 --> 00:05:38.537
-Thanks, Jimmy.

00:05:38.537 --> 00:05:40.339
-I hope to see you
in person soon.

00:05:40.339 --> 00:05:42.441
Thank you so much
for coming on the show.

00:05:42.441 --> 00:05:44.077
Cecily Strong, everybody.

